IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ma-nouns`

-- Misrepresentation Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- (plain-text rendering of the Singapore Statutes Online text; not the official text).
-- LAYER 1, SECTION 2.  Written from the text alone; nobody's point of view.
--
-- READING A RESULT.  Every decision rule answers a MAYBE BOOLEAN (or, for an amount, a MAYBE NUMBER).
-- JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the text itself gives; NOTHING means the text does not answer on
-- these facts and is never to be read as no.  Every fact is a MAYBE BOOLEAN input: NOTHING stands for a
-- fact that was not given.  Each branch that answers no carries "FALSE-kind" and the words of the text that
-- say it; each branch that answers NOTHING carries "NOTHING-kind" and why.
-- (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING".)
--
-- The Act defines no term, so "misrepresentation", "fraudulently", "loss", "reasonable ground", "equitable"
-- and what a fraudulent misrepresentation would make a person liable for (the law of deceit, not among the
-- sources) are facts the caller states.  No amount is computed: s 2 fixes no sum.

§ `Section 2 -- Damages for misrepresentation`

§§ `Section 2(1) -- liability in damages though the misrepresentation was not fraudulent`

-- src:53 | 2.—(1) Where a person has entered into a contract after a
-- src:54 | misrepresentation has been made to him by another party thereto
-- src:55 | and as a result thereof he has suffered loss, then, if the person making
-- src:56 | the misrepresentation would be liable to damages in respect thereof
-- src:57 | had the misrepresentation been made fraudulently, that person shall
-- src:58 | be so liable notwithstanding that the misrepresentation was not made
-- src:59 | fraudulently, unless he proves that he had reasonable ground to
-- src:60 | believe and did believe up to the time the contract was made that the
-- src:61 | facts represented were true.
@ref s 2(1) the proviso "unless he proves" (src:53-61)
GIVEN `he has proved that he had reasonable ground to believe that the facts represented were true` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he has proved that he did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) he proves reasonable ground to believe and belief up to the time the contract was made` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless he proves that he had reasonable ground to believe and did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true"; not proved, so this section gives no defence on these facts (it does not say there is no defence at all)
        IF `he has proved that he had reasonable ground to believe that the facts represented were true` EQUALS JUST FALSE OR `he has proved that he did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `he has proved that he had reasonable ground to believe that the facts represented were true` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `he has proved that he did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE -- both matters proved: the proviso is met
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- Fraud is not asked here.  The text makes the person liable as if the misrepresentation had been made fraudulently,
-- "notwithstanding that" it was not, so the answer is the same whether or not it was fraudulent (NOTES.md, F2).
@ref s 2(1) (src:53-61)
GIVEN `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him by another party to it` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `as a result of it he has suffered loss` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person making the misrepresentation would be liable to damages in respect of it had it been made fraudulently` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he has proved that he had reasonable ground to believe that the facts represented were true` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he has proved that he did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) the person making the misrepresentation is liable to damages` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him by another party thereto" (a "where" condition fails)
        IF `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him by another party to it` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and as a result thereof he has suffered loss" (a "where" condition fails)
        IF `as a result of it he has suffered loss` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "then, if the person making the misrepresentation would be liable to damages in respect thereof had the misrepresentation been made fraudulently" (an "if" condition fails)
        IF `the person making the misrepresentation would be liable to damages in respect of it had it been made fraudulently` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless he proves that he had reasonable ground to believe and did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true" (the liability is lifted by the proviso; a liability lifted by an "unless" is not a permission question)
        IF `s 2(1) he proves reasonable ground to believe and belief up to the time the contract was made` `he has proved that he had reasonable ground to believe that the facts represented were true` `he has proved that he did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- the text's yes: "that person shall be so liable notwithstanding that the misrepresentation was not made fraudulently"
        IF `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him by another party to it` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `as a result of it he has suffered loss` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `the person making the misrepresentation would be liable to damages in respect of it had it been made fraudulently` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `s 2(1) he proves reasonable ground to believe and belief up to the time the contract was made` `he has proved that he had reasonable ground to believe that the facts represented were true` `he has proved that he did believe up to the time the contract was made that the facts represented were true` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `Section 2(2) -- damages in lieu of rescission`

-- src:63 | (2) Where a person has entered into a contract after a
-- src:64 | misrepresentation has been made to him otherwise than
-- src:65 | fraudulently, and he would be entitled, by reason of the
-- src:66 | misrepresentation, to rescind the contract, then, if it is claimed, in
-- src:67 | any proceedings arising out of the contract, that the contract ought to
-- src:68 | be or has been rescinded, the court or arbitrator may declare the
-- src:69 | contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission, if of
-- src:70 | opinion that it would be equitable to do so, having regard to the nature
-- src:71 | of the misrepresentation and the loss that would be caused by it if the
-- src:72 | contract were upheld, as well as to the loss that rescission would
-- src:73 | cause to the other party.
@ref s 2(2) "having regard to" (src:63-73)
GIVEN `he had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he had regard to the loss that would be caused by it if the contract were upheld` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he had regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) the opinion was formed having regard to the three matters the text names` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "having regard to the nature of the misrepresentation and the loss that would be caused by it if the contract were upheld, as well as to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party" (one of the matters was not regarded)
        IF `he had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` EQUALS JUST FALSE OR `he had regard to the loss that would be caused by it if the contract were upheld` EQUALS JUST FALSE OR `he had regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `he had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `he had regard to the loss that would be caused by it if the contract were upheld` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `he had regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref s 2(2) (src:63-73)
GIVEN `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the misrepresentation was made otherwise than fraudulently` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he would be entitled, by reason of the misrepresentation, to rescind the contract` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `it is claimed, in proceedings arising out of the contract, that the contract ought to be or has been rescinded` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `who is deciding` IS A `Who is deciding`
      `it is of opinion that it would be equitable to declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `it had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `it had regard to the loss that would be caused by the misrepresentation if the contract were upheld` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `it had regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him" (a "where" condition fails)
        IF `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him otherwise than fraudulently" (a "where" condition fails: the misrepresentation was made fraudulently; the same "Where" clause as the entitlement to rescind, so the same answer)
        IF `the misrepresentation was made otherwise than fraudulently` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and he would be entitled, by reason of the misrepresentation, to rescind the contract" (a "where" condition fails)
        IF `he would be entitled, by reason of the misrepresentation, to rescind the contract` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "then, if it is claimed, in any proceedings arising out of the contract, that the contract ought to be or has been rescinded" (an "if" condition fails)
        IF `it is claimed, in proceedings arising out of the contract, that the contract ought to be or has been rescinded` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if of opinion that it would be equitable to do so" (an "if" condition fails)
        IF `it is of opinion that it would be equitable to declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is given to "the court or arbitrator" and the text names no other body
        IF `who is deciding` EQUALS `Another body`
        THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the opinion is to be formed "having regard to" the three matters the text names, and one was not regarded
        IF `s 2(2) the opinion was formed having regard to the three matters the text names` `it had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` `it had regard to the loss that would be caused by the misrepresentation if the contract were upheld` `it had regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN NOTHING
        -- the text's yes: "the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission" (a power: JUST TRUE says it may, not that it will)
        IF `he has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `the misrepresentation was made otherwise than fraudulently` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `he would be entitled, by reason of the misrepresentation, to rescind the contract` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `it is claimed, in proceedings arising out of the contract, that the contract ought to be or has been rescinded` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND (`who is deciding` EQUALS `The court` OR `who is deciding` EQUALS `An arbitrator`)
           AND `it is of opinion that it would be equitable to declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `s 2(2) the opinion was formed having regard to the three matters the text names` `it had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` `it had regard to the loss that would be caused by the misrepresentation if the contract were upheld` `it had regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- The same permission as a regulative rule.  The text names who may (the court or arbitrator) and gives no
-- time limit and no consequence, so there is no WITHIN, HENCE or LEST.  When the conditions of s 2(2) are
-- not met there is nothing to permit; FULFILLED then stands for "no permission arises here" and not for a
-- finding.  A DEONTIC value cannot be compared in an #ASSERT on the main binary: the rule is exercised with
-- #TRACE in ma-tests-s02.l4, and the decision rule above is what is asserted.
@ref s 2(2) (src:68-73)
GIVEN `who is deciding` IS A `Who is deciding`
      `the conditions of s 2(2) are met` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Who is deciding` `Step under section 2(2)`
`s 2(2) the permission of the court or arbitrator to declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission` MEANS
    IF `the conditions of s 2(2) are met` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `who is deciding` MAY `declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `Section 2(3) -- the two powers do not depend on each other; an award is taken into account`

-- src:75 | (3) Damages may be awarded against a person under subsection (2)
-- src:76 | whether or not he is liable to damages under subsection (1), but where
-- src:77 | he is so liable any award under subsection (2) shall be taken into
-- src:78 | account in assessing his liability under subsection (1).
@ref s 2(3) "whether or not he is liable to damages under subsection (1)" (src:75-78)
GIVEN `the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission under subsection (2)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) damages may be awarded under subsection (2) whether or not he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` MEANS
    -- The answer is the answer under subsection (2), whatever the answer under subsection (1): the text says
    -- that liability under subsection (1) is not a condition of the power in subsection (2).
    `the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission under subsection (2)`

@ref s 2(3) "where he is so liable any award under subsection (2) shall be taken into account" (src:75-78)
GIVEN `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `an award has been made under subsection (2)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) an award under subsection (2) is to be taken into account in assessing his liability under subsection (1)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but where he is so liable" (a "where" condition fails: not liable under subsection (1))
        IF `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `an award has been made under subsection (2)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE -- the text's yes: "any award under subsection (2) shall be taken into account in assessing his liability under subsection (1)"
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rule is about "any award under subsection (2)"; where none has been made there is no award to take into account
        IF `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND `an award has been made under subsection (2)` EQUALS JUST FALSE
        THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a fact the answer turns on was not given (an input is NOTHING), and the text decides nothing without it
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref s 2(3) "taken into account" (src:77-78)
GIVEN `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `an award has been made under subsection (2)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the amount of the award made under subsection (2)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 2(3) the amount of the award under subsection (2) that is to be taken into account` MEANS
    IF `s 2(3) an award under subsection (2) is to be taken into account in assessing his liability under subsection (1)` `he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` `an award has been made under subsection (2)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN `the amount of the award made under subsection (2)`
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the rule above does not apply or could not be decided; and where it does apply the text says only "taken into account", not "deducted", so no net liability under subsection (1) is worked out here
    ELSE NOTHING
